Equality, hashing, and collection contracts.

Hash-set, hash-map, and their tree cousins are only as correct as the elements you put in them. They quietly depend on equals, hash code, and — for the tree kinds — compare-to, all obeying their contracts. Get those right, and collections just work. Get them wrong, and you'll chase bugs where a key you clearly inserted "isn't there." This section makes that dependency explicit.

Start with how a hash map actually finds a key. A hash map is a bank of buckets, and to locate a key it does two steps. First, it computes the key's hash code, to pick a bucket — that's fast, jumping near the right place. Then, it walks that bucket, comparing with equals, to find the exact key. So both methods are load-bearing: hash code decides where to look, and equals decides which one it is. This is why the module-two rule is absolute here — equal objects must have equal hash codes. Break it, and a key can hash to bucket A, while its equal twin hashes to bucket B; the lookup checks the wrong bucket and reports "absent".

Let's make the failure modes concrete. First: you overrode equals, but not hash code. Now two equal keys get different buckets. Map dot get of an equal key returns null, and a hash-set stores visible duplicates. That's the single most common collections bug. Second: you overrode neither, so you get the default identity behaviour. Every distinct object is its own key, so a new point of one, one never matches another new point of one, one. You fix both by making value types records, which generate a correct pair for you. Third: a bad hash code — say, returning zero for every key. Now everything lands in one bucket, and the map degrades from constant time to order-n — a linear scan wearing a hash map's clothing.

There's also the mutable-key trap. Even with correct methods, mutating a key after inserting it breaks the map. Suppose you put an array-list as a key, mapped to some value. Then you add an element to that list. Its hash code just changed, so the entry is now in the wrong bucket. Map dot get of that same key returns null — the entry is effectively lost. It sits in the bucket for its old hash, but lookups compute the new one. So the rule is: keys, and set elements, must be immutable — or at least never mutated in a way that changes hash code or equals. This is the deepest reason to reach for records and string as keys.

The tree contract is different, because it uses comparison. Tree-map and tree-set don't call equals or hash code at all — they decide both ordering and equality by compare-to, or a comparator. Two elements are the same, to a tree, when comparison returns zero. So an ordering that's inconsistent with equals makes a tree-set behave oddly. Big-decimal of "one point zero" and big-decimal of "one point zero-zero" are not equal, yet compare-to says they're equal — so put both in a tree-set, and it keeps only one. The rule: for tree collections, keep compare-to consistent with equals — compare-to returns zero exactly when equals is true — or know precisely why you're deviating.

So the takeaway. These contracts aren't academic — they're the reason your data structures find what you stored. And two habits cover almost everything: make value types records, for correct equals and hash code for free; and keep keys and set-elements immutable. For tree-based collections, additionally keep the ordering consistent with equality. Honour that, and hashing and sorting are invisible machinery. Ignore it, and they become the hardest bugs you'll meet. With the contracts clear, the final section pulls the whole module together: choosing the right collection.
